para('What this resolves',h2);para('The policy is responsive to context rather than uniformly reluctant to change: neutral matched cases held in 31/32, while genuine aerial/ground mismatches triggered changes. Hold remains valid. A tactical label or a single lost counterfactual is not an oracle of the best expected action. The prospectively fixed interval allows heterogeneous independent streams; all context and recipe splits are descriptive.')
para('The workload cases used native physics and recovery with stamina 2/recovery 1, and either 20 or 35 eligible players. All 96 focal teams in those extreme cases lost under both arms. That gives useful workload coverage, but saturated losses cannot calibrate win-rate value. No case retained median active energy below 0.5 at the decision point, so the low-energy pressing veto still lacks natural-match coverage.',sm)
story.append(PageBreak());para('Concrete defect, correction and next gate',h)
para('Boundary support was misattributed',h2);para(f"In <b>{boundary['affectedDecisionCount']} decisions</b>, the policy selected an aerial-direction recipe even though aerial emphasis was already at its limit. Only ball risk changed, but the recipe retained full aerial-direction support. Two deterministic probes reproduced both limits. This is an action-support defect, not proof that the risk-only action harmed results. It does <b>not explain the earlier five adverse cases</b>, whose aerial roots were interior.")
para('A separate candidate, <b>restored-halftime-policy-v2-boundary</b>, makes a fully blocked aerial recipe inadmissible in automatic and diagnostic modes. For a partially clipped aerial change, response gain follows the actual aerial movement. Adoption thresholds, numeric cost coefficients, other recipes, personnel, physics and recovery are unchanged. The evaluated v1 source and outcomes remain intact.')
